{
  "note": "Glia in Synaptogenesis and Syniaptic Activity",
  "source": "5773f47e43f1",
  "script": "7c9cecd7a75f",
  "voice": "Jessica Gallagher - Clear and Neutral",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "ed0b2674d2bd",
  "duration": 214.544,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Glia in Synaptogenesis and Synaptic Activity",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Glia are not passive support cells, but active partners at the synapse.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 5.28
        },
        {
          "text": "In the central nervous system, or C N S, synapses only form and persist when astrocytes or other macroglia are present.",
          "start": 5.36,
          "end": 14
        },
        {
          "text": "On top of that, glia sense and respond to ongoing synaptic activity.",
          "start": 14.08,
          "end": 19.16
        },
        {
          "text": "In what follows, we'll look first at how glia help synapses form, and then at the part they play in synaptic activity.",
          "start": 19.2,
          "end": 26.88
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "1. Role of glia in synaptogenesis",
      "start": 27.638,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "We'll begin with development, when neurons migrate into position.",
          "start": 27.638,
          "end": 32.078
        },
        {
          "text": "Yet synapse formation only occurs when astrocytes or other macroglia are present.",
          "start": 32.118,
          "end": 37.691
        },
        {
          "text": "The astrocytes release extracellular protein signals, and these signals trigger synapse formation in the C N S.",
          "start": 37.745,
          "end": 44.878
        },
        {
          "text": "This dependence isn't limited to development, either.",
          "start": 44.918,
          "end": 48.158
        },
        {
          "text": "Adult hippocampal stem cells need astrocytes to form synapses as well.",
          "start": 48.198,
          "end": 53.038
        },
        {
          "text": "Even once synapses are built, they still rely on glia.",
          "start": 53.078,
          "end": 56.958
        },
        {
          "text": "That's because macroglia are necessary for synapse maintenance, not only for formation.",
          "start": 56.998,
          "end": 62.518
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "2. Macroglia actively control synaptogenesis",
      "start": 63.295,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Crucially, the glial control of synapse formation is active rather than permissive.",
          "start": 63.295,
          "end": 69.295
        },
        {
          "text": "Astrocytes and other macroglia regulate synapse number, synapse stability and synapse function.",
          "start": 69.375,
          "end": 76.455
        },
        {
          "text": "Nor does this principle stop at the C N S.",
          "start": 76.495,
          "end": 79.415
        },
        {
          "text": "There is evidence that Schwann cells, out in the periphery, trigger the formation of the neuromuscular junction.",
          "start": 79.455,
          "end": 86.015
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "3. Role in synaptic activity",
      "start": 86.779,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Turning to synaptic activity, glia take part in ongoing signalling too.",
          "start": 86.779,
          "end": 92.019
        },
        {
          "text": "In practice, the glia involved are astrocytes, as has been shown in culture.",
          "start": 92.059,
          "end": 97.219
        },
        {
          "text": "They can do this because glial cells carry neurotransmitter receptors, mostly metabotropic ones that are coupled to intracellular cascades.",
          "start": 97.259,
          "end": 106.352
        },
        {
          "text": "When transmitter is released during synaptic transmission, the glial cell responds as well.",
          "start": 106.406,
          "end": 112.112
        },
        {
          "text": "Its response shows up as calcium transient currents, which are brief rises of intracellular calcium inside the glial cell.",
          "start": 112.166,
          "end": 120.326
        },
        {
          "text": "These transients are the measurable sign that the glia has sensed synaptic activity.",
          "start": 120.432,
          "end": 125.619
        },
        {
          "text": "From there, the calcium works as a second messenger within the glial cell.",
          "start": 125.659,
          "end": 130.259
        },
        {
          "text": "The signal can even propagate as a calcium wave, both within one glial cell and between neighbouring glia.",
          "start": 130.299,
          "end": 137.619
        },
        {
          "text": "As for the purpose of that calcium signal, it triggers the glial cell to release gliotransmitters in response to neuronal activity.",
          "start": 137.659,
          "end": 145.859
        },
        {
          "text": "What these gliotransmitters signal back onto is the synapse itself.",
          "start": 145.899,
          "end": 150.579
        },
        {
          "text": "They can regulate how much transmitter the presynaptic terminal releases, or alter the responsiveness of the postsynaptic cell.",
          "start": 150.619,
          "end": 158.579
        },
        {
          "text": "Through this, they can also influence activity-dependent plastic changes.",
          "start": 158.619,
          "end": 163.472
        },
        {
          "text": "Put another way, synaptic signalling is not a private dialogue between two neurons.",
          "start": 163.526,
          "end": 169.072
        },
        {
          "text": "The glial cell listens, and talks back to tune the transmission.",
          "start": 169.126,
          "end": 173.579
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Summary",
      "start": 174.341,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "So, pulling the threads together, astrocytes and other macroglia are required for both the formation and the maintenance of C N S synapses.",
          "start": 174.341,
          "end": 183.941
        },
        {
          "text": "They drive formation through extracellular protein signals, and they actively regulate synapse number, stability and function.",
          "start": 184.021,
          "end": 192.141
        },
        {
          "text": "Schwann cells play the equivalent role at the neuromuscular junction.",
          "start": 192.181,
          "end": 196.221
        },
        {
          "text": "Beyond that, glia sense synaptic activity through calcium transients.",
          "start": 196.261,
          "end": 201.061
        },
        {
          "text": "They answer it by releasing gliotransmitters, which makes them active participants at the synapse.",
          "start": 201.141,
          "end": 207.088
        },
        {
          "text": "And with that, we've seen how glia help synapses form and last, and how they join in the conversation across them.",
          "start": 207.114,
          "end": 214.581
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
