    {"duration":87.562449,"alignment":{"characters":"Turning to synaptic activity, glia take part in ongoing signalling too. In practice, the glia involved are astrocytes, as has been shown in culture. They can do this because glial cells carry neurotransmitter receptors, mostly metabotropic ones that are coupled to intracellular cascades. When transmitter is released during synaptic transmission, the glial cell responds as well. Its response shows up as calcium transient currents, which are brief rises of intracellular calcium inside the glial cell. These transients are the measurable sign that the glia has sensed synaptic activity. From there, the calcium works as a second messenger within the glial cell. The signal can even propagate as a calcium wave, both within one glial cell and between neighbouring glia. As for the purpose of that calcium signal, it triggers the glial cell to release gliotransmitters in response to neuronal activity. What these gliotransmitters signal back onto is the synapse itself. They can regulate how much transmitter the presynaptic terminal releases, or alter the responsiveness of the postsynaptic cell. Through this, they can also influence activity-dependent plastic changes. Put another way, synaptic signalling is not a private dialogue between two neurons. The glial cell listens, and talks back to tune the transmission.","starts":[0,46,91,137,183,229,274,320,400,440,480,600,640,680,720,784,848,912,976,1040,1320,1355,1390,1425,1460,1495,1530,1565,1600,2000,2080,2120,2160,2280,2400,2520,2550,2580,2610,2640,2720,2780,2840,2900,2960,3040,3120,3200,3280,3360,3440,3520,3600,3653,3707,3760,3840,3880,3920,3960,4000,4053,4107,4160,4213,4267,4320,4400,4667,4933,5200,5240,5280,5320,5360,5440,5520,5600,5680,5760,5840,5920,6000,6080,6120,6160,6187,6213,6240,6320,6360,6400,6480,6560,6640,6720,6800,6880,6928,6976,7024,7072,7120,7200,7253,7307,7360,7440,7504,7568,7632,7696,7760,7840,7920,8053,8187,8320,8360,8400,8480,8560,8640,8667,8693,8720,8800,88